<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Enniskillen</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Enniskillen is the county town of Fermanagh in Northern Ireland, on an island between Upper and Lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lough-erne">Lough Erne</a>. Growing around the 15th-century castle of the Maguires, it became a plantation town and Protestant garrison in the 17th century and held out for William III in 1689.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/enniskillen.jpg" alt="Enniskillen" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Raymond Millar · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Enniskillen_Castle.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Enniskillen is the largest town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-fermanagh">County Fermanagh</a>, Northern Ireland, in the middle of the county between the Upper and Lower sections of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lough-erne">Lough Erne</a>. Its name comes from the Irish Inis Ceithleann, the island of Ceithlenn. Ceithlenn is a figure in Irish mythology who may have been a goddess, and local legend says she was wounded in battle by an arrow and tried to swim the river Erne, which surrounds the island, but never reached the far side. The name has been anglicised in many ways over the centuries, among them Iniskellen, Inishkillen and Iniskillin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The oldest building is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enniskillen-castle">Enniskillen Castle</a>, built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-maguire-lord-of-fermanagh">Hugh Maguire</a> the Hospitable, who died in 1428, as the stronghold of a junior branch of the Maguires. The first watergate was built around 1580 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cu-chonnacht-maguire">Cú Chonnacht Maguire</a>. The English wanted the castle to support their plans for control of Ulster, and in February 1594 a force led by a Captain Dowdall captured it. Maguire then besieged it and defeated a relieving force at the Battle of the Ford of the Biscuits. He held the castle from 1595 to 1598, and the English finally took it in 1607.</p><p>The capture was followed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plantation-of-ulster">Plantation of Ulster</a>, in which native Irish lands were seized and given to planters loyal to the Crown. William Cole, originally from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devon">Devon</a>, was appointed by James I to build an English settlement there in 1612. He became constable, strengthened the castle wall and built a house on the old foundation. The first Protestant parish church was built on the hilltop in 1627, and by 1630 the town had about 180 inhabitants, mostly English and Scottish settlers. Permanent bridges were not installed before 1688.</p><p>In the conflict that followed the ousting of James II by William III, Enniskillen and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derry">Derry</a> were the focus of Williamite resistance in Ireland, including the nearby Battle of Newtownbutler. Enniskillen was the last town to fall before the Siege of Derry. As a result it became a garrison as well as a market town, and two regiments of the British Army were founded there, the Inniskilling Dragoons and the Royal Inniskilling Fusiliers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Enniskillen is the county town of Fermanagh and had a population of 14,086 at the 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. Its castle, Castle Coole and the nearby Florence Court are among its historic buildings, and St Macartin's Cathedral dates from 1840. In November 1987 a bombing at a Remembrance Day ceremony killed 11 people.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Enniskillen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Fermanagh was then planted with English and Scottish settlers, and <b>Enniskillen</b> became a Protestant garrison town.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-fermanagh">Kingdom of Fermanagh</a></li></ul></section>
